    <DataNode elementId="AT5G63910-MONOMER" textLabel="farnesylcysteine lyase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="P57681" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">Farnesylcysteine lyase (FCLY) can act to begin the recycling of |FRAME: CPD-12581 &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-(2&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;,6&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;)-farnesyl-L-cysteine| (FC) residues that have been cleaved off the C-terminal end of prenylated proteins when they are degraded. Two &lt;i&gt;fcly&lt;/i&gt; mutants have reduced levels of FC lyase activity.These mutants also have increased sensitivity to ABA suggesting that elevated levels of FC in these mutants might be serving as competitive inhibitors of the prenylcysteine methyltransferases that normally down-regulate ABA signaling |CITS: [17425716]|. In addition to characterizing mutants, experiments were performed using Arabidopsis membranes. These revealed the presence of a protein with FC lyase activity with an apparent specific activity of 156 +/- 11 pmol min &lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; mg &lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; |CITS: [17425716]|. This activity could be detected in cultured cells, seedlings, flowers, stems, and roots |CITS: [17425716]|. Competition assays using membrane fractions and |FRAME: CPD-12591 all-&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-geranylgeranylcysteine| (GGC) or geranylcysteine demonstrated a strong preference for |FRAME: CPD-12581 &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-(2&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;,6&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;)-farnesyl-L-cysteine| |CITS: [17425716]|. This contrasts with a purified prenylcysteine lyase identified from bovine serum that has similar activity using both |FRAME: CPD-12581 FC| and |FRAME: CPD-12591 GGC| |CITS: [9287348]|. In addition, the Arabidopsis FC lyase found in membrane fractions acts on |FRAME: CPD-12592 N-acetyl-farnesylcysteine|, a model substrate that mimics the farnesylated C-terminal residue on a protein. This activity suggests that the enzyme may be able to de-farnesylate the farnesylated cysteine residues that are part of prenylated proteins. |CITS: [17425716]|. However, because all of these experiments were done using unpurified Arabidopsis membranes, these enzymatic activities and characteristics could not be conclusively linked to the FCLY encoded by the Arabidopsis gene |FRAME: AT5G63910 AT5G63910|. Therefore, further work was done using FCLY expresessed in &lt;i&gt;Sf&lt;/i&gt;9 cells. This protein was N-glycoslyated when translated in the presence of microsomal membrane fractions &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; which can explain the discrepancy between the predicted molecular weight and the weight calculated based on migration of the purified enzyme on an SDS-PAGE gel |CITS: [19969520]|. The recombinant protein showed direct activity on FC &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; and also showed inhibition by |FRAME: CPD-12592 N-acetyl-farnesylcysteine| and farnesyl homocysteine. Again, |FRAME: CPD-12591 all-&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-geranylgeranylcysteine| had no effect on the activity of FC lyase suggesting that this is not a substrate of the enzyme &lt;i&gt;in planta&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [19969520]|. This enzyme is likely to be present in vacuolar membranes based on proteomic experiments |CITS: [15539469][17151019]| and the identification of two potential vacuolar sorting signals |CITS: [19969520]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G63910-MONOMER" />
